  "explain": [
    {
      "say": "Pump water up a long pipe and you lose some of the push to friction on the walls. Engineers measure that loss as head, the height of water it could have lifted. Henry Darcy and Julius Weisbach gave us the formula in the 1840s and 50s. Look at the energy bar: part of the pump head goes to friction.",
      "draw": [
        {
          "op": "clear"
        },
        {
          "op": "title",
          "text": "Pipe friction and head loss with the Darcy–Weisbach equation"
        },
        {
          "name": "x-energy",
          "input": {
            "label": "Pump head",
            "amount": 20
          },
          "unit": "m",
          "outputs": [
            {
              "label": "Lift to tank",
              "amount": 15,
              "useful": true
            },
            {
              "label": "Pipe friction",
              "amount": 3.7,
              "useful": false
            },
            {
              "label": "Bends and valves",
              "amount": 1.3,
              "useful": false
            }
          ],
          "op": "sim",
          "focus": 1
        },
        {
          "op": "note",
          "text": "20 m of pump head: 15 m lift, 5 m lost"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "say": "The Darcy–Weisbach equation says head loss equals f, times length over diameter, times V squared over 2 g. Longer pipe, more loss. Thinner pipe, more loss. And double the speed means four times the loss. The friction factor f is the tricky part. Which change hurts more, doubling the length or doubling the speed?",
      "draw": [
        {
          "name": "x-energy",
          "input": {
            "label": "Pump head",
            "amount": 20
          },
          "unit": "m",
          "outputs": [
